# migration-paranoid

Static analyzer for risky database migrations.

Warns about migrations that can lock tables, drop data, or break rolling deploys.

## Supported

- PostgreSQL
- MySQL
- SQLite
- Prisma
- Rails (ActiveRecord migrations)
- Django
- Flyway (`.sql` migrations)
- Liquibase (XML / YAML / JSON changelogs)

## Why it helps

Bad migrations are one of the easiest ways to cause production incidents. This
tool scans your migrations directory and flags the dangerous patterns *before*
they reach production.

## Install

```bash
pip install .
```

Or run without installing:

```bash
PYTHONPATH=src python -m migration_paranoid db/migrations
```

## Usage

```bash
migration-paranoid db/migrations
```

Example output:

```text
🚨 db/migrations/202604301200_add_not_null_to_users.sql:2  [SQL001] SET NOT NULL on users.email

  ALTER TABLE users ALTER COLUMN email SET NOT NULL;

Risk:
- May scan full table.
- May fail if existing rows contain NULL.
- Unsafe during rolling deploy unless app already writes email.

Suggestion: On Postgres 12+: add a CHECK (col IS NOT NULL) NOT VALID, VALIDATE CONSTRAINT, then ALTER COLUMN SET NOT NULL.

Summary: 1 finding(s) — 1 high
```

### Options

| Flag | Description |
| --- | --- |
| `--format {text,json}` | Output format. JSON is machine-readable for CI integrations. |
| `--min-severity {info,low,medium,high,critical}` | Hide findings below this severity. |
| `--fail-on {info,low,medium,high,critical}` | Exit non-zero if any finding meets this severity (default: `medium`). |
| `-V`, `--version` | Print version. |

The CLI exits with code `1` when at least one finding meets `--fail-on`, so it
plugs straight into CI:

```yaml
- run: migration-paranoid db/migrations
```

## Rules

### SQL (PostgreSQL / MySQL / SQLite / Flyway / Prisma migrations)

| ID | Pattern | Severity |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `SQL001` | `ALTER ... SET NOT NULL` | high |
| `SQL002` | `ADD COLUMN ... NOT NULL` without `DEFAULT` | high |
| `SQL003` | `DROP COLUMN` | high |
| `SQL004` | `DROP TABLE` | critical |
| `SQL005` | `ALTER COLUMN ... TYPE` | high |
| `SQL006` | `RENAME COLUMN` | medium |
| `SQL007` | `RENAME TABLE` | medium |
| `SQL008` | `UPDATE` without `WHERE` | high |
| `SQL009` | `DELETE` without `WHERE` | critical |
| `SQL010` | `TRUNCATE` | high |
| `PG001` | `CREATE INDEX` without `CONCURRENTLY` (Postgres) | high |

### Rails

`RAILS001` `remove_column` · `RAILS002` `drop_table` ·
`RAILS003` `change_column_null ..., false` · `RAILS004` `add_index` without
`algorithm: :concurrently` · `RAILS005` `rename_column` ·
`RAILS006` `rename_table` · `RAILS007` `change_column` ·
`RAILS008` `add_column ... null: false` without `default:`

### Django

`DJANGO001` `RemoveField` · `DJANGO002` `DeleteModel` ·
`DJANGO003` `RenameField` · `DJANGO004` `RenameModel` ·
`DJANGO005` `RunSQL` (review hint).

### Liquibase

`LB001` `dropColumn` · `LB002` `dropTable` · `LB003` `renameColumn` ·
`LB004` `renameTable` · `LB005` `addNotNullConstraint` ·
`LB006` `modifyDataType`.

## Development

```bash
python -m unittest discover -s tests
```
